  v9-splash vs Splash stock: 7-5, legs split. Parity;
  U18 stays open.

Two readings, both recorded. First, the local one: the
champion package is a martial instrument — it reprices
minion swings toward the hero, and Mage's minions are
utility (bounce, tokens, draw engines) whose value is
not in their swings. Face pressure on a Mage deck is a
category error, and the arena charged full price for it.

Second, the field reading: in both series the random
veteran seat took 70-85% of episodes — far above its
25-45% in Archer/Warrior company. Mage and Splash stocks
are simply soft fields to farm, which matches the class
map (Round 12) and warns against reading any Mage-deck
result without the veteran baseline beside it.

The 11D map gains a boundary: the package's region is
Archer + Warrior (verified), Splash (neutral), Mage
(harmful). Evolution continues along the boundary, not
through it: the next Mage point, if any, needs a heuristic
native to utility minions — a design problem, entered on
the frontier list, not an import.ROUND 17 — the flux capacitor: lessons of the False shelf (EE 388)

Will's direction: learn from the failures, 88 mph, flux
capacitor. The False shelf is the capacitor — seven kept,
receipted refutations store the charge that lets the
programme jump instead of restarting at zero each round.
Distilled, one lesson per failure:

  F1 (v2, flat-100 lethal): a uniform price on a
  conditional good spends your best swings on dying
  heroes. LESSON: price conditionally or don't price.
  F3 (v3b, neighbor lethal): the ring is not a duel;
  enemyPlayer rotates. LESSON: never optimize for a
  geometry the arena doesn't have.
  F4 (v3a, elimination at 25): tested to a dead heat —
  a price that fires everywhere decides nowhere.
  LESSON: a heuristic's value is in its selectivity.
  F5 (v1, the founding claim): the "advance" was seat-0
  noise. LESSON: anchor on the honest baseline; most
  advances are measurement artifacts until seat-balanced.
  F6 (the blanket veteran claim): true for one tribe,
  false for the other. LESSON: partition before you
  generalize — the average of two tribes is a fiction.
  F7 (package on Mage): repricing swings harms a deck
  whose minions aren't swingers. LESSON: a heuristic has
  a native substrate; ask what the pieces are FOR first.

Applied at speed, the same hour: v10-mage-long
(emise-cac-v10-mage-long:v1, policy 5fba5f86) — a
Mage-native package built from F7 + F1: hero swings at
stock patience (4 + 2p) unless ahead on board
(selfBoardSize >= enemyBoardSize -> 10 + 2p), lethal
always 100. One hunk; deck byte-identical. Claim U19
entered at design time; 40 episodes running
(xreq_f7c88ef0, xreq_ac90de51) against the honest
baseline, Mage stock — per F5.

88 mph is a method, not a mood: the failures were
already paid for; the lessons compound only if spent.ROUND 18 — the Long Game is neutral; the harm is repaired (EE 389)

v10's receipts, inside the hour (40 episodes vs Mage
stock): 4-4 over the 8 decisive episodes, legs split
1-3 and 3-1, the veteran seat farming 32 of 40 as it
did in Round 16's series.

Read plainly: the F7-derived design did exactly half
its job. The board-gate removed the poison — where the
imported package won 0, the native Long Game matches
its stock twin win for win. But parity is not an edge,
and U19 stays open with the result recorded as a
finding: on the Mage deck, swing pricing is not where
the game is decided. The capacitor's next charge points
at the two untested Mage levers — the deck itself
(Oozification/Morphosis/Primordial counts against the
flat precon) and the card-play scorer, which no variant
in the lineage has ever touched.

That last fact is the round's real yield: eleven
variants in, every change has lived in swing or the
deck list. The score function — what to play, when —
is the lineage's largest unexplored room.ROUND 19 — the past at full speed: the headless runner stands (EE 395)

Will's direction — evolve faster in the past — is now
machinery. Nim 2.4.2 stands on 19 ranch nodes (choosenim,
user-local; dossier: NIM-DOSSIER.md), its source held at
/source/Nim on the hub and in Will's fork wbic16/Nim
(v2.4.2 tag pushed from the hub clone). polyworld is
cloned on aletheia-core and the headless runner BUILT:
games/cac/build/cac-headless (1.78 MB).

The build's one real fight, recorded so it is never
re-fought: nimble's registry pinned bassy to a stale
commit (99974660) that lacks ContextHostProc, which
polyworld HEAD requires. Fix: nimble install
bassy@#a9effca — the committish form — after which the
