Validation against hosted receipts (the screen earns
trust before it spends it), 100 seeds each, 5 seats,
fifth seat the reference warrior bot:
  A (v1 vs starter): v1 46, starter 36, ref 18 —
     parity-shaped, matching the hosted seat-balanced
     dead heat (F5) within noise.
  B (v6 vs starter): v6 73, starter 5, ref 22 — the
     champion's hosted dominance (T9, T10) reproduces
     at full strength.
Throughput: ~0.05 s per match, ~19 matches/sec on one
hub core — roughly four orders of magnitude faster
than hosted episodes, at zero platform metering.

Standing caveats, riding with every local number: the
local first move is random where the hosted ring's
seat order is fixed (T6 does not transfer); the local
fifth seat is a reference bot, not a veteran; local
results SCREEN, hosted results DECIDE. The past is now
re-runnable: every variant in the lineage can be
re-tested in thousands of seeded matches before a
single hosted episode is spent.ROUND 20 — 105,000 matches: the matrix and the equivalence classes (EE 397)

The full round-robin, run at ranch pace on Will's word:
15 lineage bots, every pair, both seat-swapped legs,
500 seeds a leg — 105,000 matches across 19 nodes in
under two minutes of wall time. 100% decided. (First
attempt played zero: a roster-argument bug; caught,
named, re-run with a zero-decision guard — the tally
now records ERROR instead of zeros.)

LOCAL FITNESS (win share of decisive pair outcomes):
v6 87.4, v7 84.9, v5-team 75.5, v3c 54.7, v4-warrior
49.9, starter 44.0, v1 43.8, v2 43.0, v3a 42.5, v3b
42.1, v8-mage 35.7, v9-splash 35.0, v10-mage 34.1,
mage-stock 27.7, splash-stock 20.9.

CALIBRATION: hosted verdicts reproduce where the
mechanism binds — T10 (v6 over starter 837-61), T7,
T12, T8 (v5 over its own base v3c, 526-270), U8's
direction (Warrior over starter 508-292). Two honest
exceptions: the local Mage/Splash fields are
reference-dominated (ref took 63-69% of those pairs),
and F7's harm does NOT reproduce locally (v8 beat
mage-stock 202-140; hosted it won 0) — the package's
poison needs the veteran field to express itself.

THE EQUIVALENCE FINDING (the round's real yield):
several pairs returned byte-identical tallies.
Verified at match level: v2 and v3b produce IDENTICAL
winner sequences against v1 over 200 seeds, while
their game lengths differ (17 of 200 lines differ in
ticks/turns vs starter). Bots whose only differences
are lethal-pricing branches change how games unfold
far more readily than who wins them — in this field,
the lethal swing is usually already the best play at
stock prices, so the premium rarely binds. Consequence,
recorded as method: the local screen CANNOT adjudicate
lethal-pricing claims (U3, the F1/F4/F5 family) — those
stay hosted-only. It screens decks, face pressure,
team protocol, and board gates.

SCREEN READINGS (evidence class: screen; no registry
transitions): U16 — v6 516, v7 436 (n=952): the reach
deck is not an advance over the champion on either
surface. U19 — v10 176, mage-stock 131: the Long Game
gate helps locally. U18 — v9 309, splash-stock 58:
package strongly positive in a soft local field;
hosted parity stands until hosted evidence moves.Round 21 — Composition (EE 417)
Will asked: run another round — do you learn more? Yes, when the round is aimed at what the last one could not see. Three questions, 45,000 matches, three rotating field flavors (Warrior/Archer/Mage reference in the fifth seat), per-seat recording, zero errors.

v11 — THE COMPOSITION. The champion package and the team protocol are the lineage's two strongest mechanisms; v11 is v6 plus the protocol, ported hunk-for-hunk (the v6-to-v11 diff is exactly the protocol's 47 lines, verified). Screen verdict: v11 beats v6 head-to-head 1,911-1,055 over 2,966 decisive pair outcomes (64%), and the margin holds in every field flavor — Warrior 620-364, Archer 662-332, Mage 629-359. v11 tops the fitness table at ~80% in all three flavors. Registered as U20 (Unknown): the screen ran in the pair format where partners are guaranteed and correctly seated; a hosted pair series decides, and a lone league seat would gain nothing from the protocol.

FIELD-STABILITY. The fitness order — v11, v6, v7, v5-team, v3c, starter — is identical in all three field flavors. Locally, the ranking is not a field artifact. (The F7 exception stands apart: the package's harm on Mage needed the veteran field specifically, not just a different reference bot.)

SEAT GEOMETRY, MEASURED LOCALLY. Pooled over 45,000 matches: seat 0 won 30.8%, seats 1-3 won 21.9/20.0/19.6%, the field seat 7.7%. A seat-0 premium exists on this surface too, at roughly half the hosted magnitude — turn order inside the round, not only first move.

SUBSTRATE SPEED, MEASURED (Will: look for substrate speedups; EE 417). Release flags do not help: a -d:danger --opt:speed build ran 5-9% SLOWER than the stock build in clean sequential and parallel tests — the stock build stands. Match length: median 173 ticks, p99 244, max 296 in 500 sampled — the 3,000-tick cap is a tail-trimmer for pathological matchups, not a speed lever. Rates: ~129 matches/sec/node at 14 slots (per-match cost nearly doubles under parallel load — contention, ~55% scaling efficiency). The remaining lever is structural: an in-process batch driver looping matches inside one process, killing per-match process spawn and sharing loaded programs. Not yet built.

THE ONE-MINUTE RULE (Will, EE 417): every round plays as many matches as fit in one minute at ranch pace. Calibration at current orchestration: ~60,000 matches end-to-end (Round 20's proven 105,000 in 106 s); pure compute ceiling ~147,000/min. Rounds are sized to the minute from here.
Round 22 — Interactions (EE 419)
56,500 matches, zero errors, sized to the one-minute rule (103 s wall end-to-end; orchestration remains ~40 s of that — the batch driver is still the untaken lever). Three questions.

THE PROTOCOL HAS A NATIVE DECK. v12 — champion package + team protocol + v7's reach deck — was built to test whether the deck axis adds at the top. It does the opposite: v11 beat v12 8,020-5,233 pooled (v12 took 39.5% of decisive), consistently across all three field flavors, while v12 still beat v6 (55.5%) and v7 (61.1%). The order is v11, v12, v6, v7. Composition is not additive: the protocol converts width into focused damage, and the reach deck trims exactly the width cards (Banner, Footsoldier, Commander at full sets) to pay for Bolt and the Archer trinket. F7's lesson echoes one level up — a mechanism has a native substrate; the protocol's substrate is a wide Warrior board. Registered as U21.

U20 HARDENS. v11 vs v6 with v7 — a lineage bot, not a reference — in the fifth seat: 2,356-1,245, 65.4% of decisive, against 64.4% under reference fields. The protocol's margin is not a soft-field artifact on the local surface. Hosted pair series still decides the claim.

v13 AND THE CARD NOT READ. The Mage deck swap (Burnout in, Plan/Control trimmed) collapsed: 36 wins in 1,198 decisive against mage-stock, ~3% against every Mage sibling, the field taking ~70%. The mechanism, read at baseset.nim after the fact: Burnout is a 0-cost Mage trinket that deals 2 damage to its owner and tosses a card at each of the owner's turn-ends — the punishment token Overwork leaves behind when destroyed. It sits in the deckable base set, and the flat precon excludes it because playing it is self-harm by installments; the stock scorer plays it anyway. The design error is recorded plainly: the swap was designed from the card's absence in the precon, without reading its rules text. The failure shelf gains its shortest entry: read the card. Registered as U22.
Round 23 — Saturation (EE 424)
57,000 matches, zero errors, 65 seconds wall — the one-minute rule met end-to-end for the first time. Two one-line probes on the champion composition: v14 raises the protocol's focus-fire swing bonus from 3 to 6; v15 cuts the chip-swing value from 6 to 2 (swing at minions only when it kills or survives profitably).

Both probes landed in the champion's equivalence class. Against v11: v14 took 49.6% of 13,331 decisive, v15 took 49.7% — parity. Against v6 both took 63.0%, v11's own mark. And their tallies against shared opponents are near-identical to each other (one pair byte-identical; the rest within 3 matches in 4,500) — the same signature Round 20 found for lethal pricing, now on the protocol's own tuning knobs. The mechanism is the same: a price that almost never changes the argmax is not a lever. At +3 the focus target was already the chosen swing nearly everywhere; chip swings were already the last resort.

The finding is the boundary of an era: at the champion point, the pricing surface is saturated. Fifteen variants in, every price we know how to turn has been turned. What remains is structural — the score function (which card to play; never yet modified by any variant), the protocol's shape (its focus definition, its partner geometry), a Mage-native scorer — or hosted evidence on the claims the screen cannot move (U20 above all). Registered as U23 and U24.
Round 24 — The Champion Succession (EE 430)
Will: submit new champion. The new champion by evidence is v11 — and v11 does not enter the arena in its pair-format form. Its partner arithmetic (selfPlayer +/- 2) assumes our test rosters; in a lone league seat it would designate a stranger as partner: that opponent gains partial immunity from our harmful targeting while outsiders live, and our helpful effects may land on their minions. The protocol's pair half is roster-geometry; it stays home.

What enters is v16, the league form: v11 with one hunk changed — partner forced to -1, so no one is protected and no stranger is buffed — keeping the half of the protocol that needs no partner: focus-fire on the highest-life outsider, priced into both swing (+3) and card targeting (+2), over the champion package on the flat Warrior deck.

The adaptation was screened before it was submitted, in the format it will actually play: lone seats, rotating through all five positions, a field of v6 and the three reference bots, 10,000 matches. v16 took 52.1% of wins; v6 took 33.2%; the references took 1.8-9.5%. Fair share is 20%.

Uploaded as emise-cac-v16:v1 (policy version adae4ee8-c75c-40ed-9585-2a6963bac8fa) and submitted to league_ad6dc809 on Will's word: submission sub_8d445f1, auto-champion always, placement pending at inscription. v6 (sub_77d6ffb1, placed) holds the seat until v16's placement settles. The daily arena sweep now tracks v16 as champion.
Round 25 — Singular, Our Own Coworld (EE 431-449)
Will chose the third road: design a new phext-native game from scratch. His rulings (EE 432): the name is Singular; the tagline is "magic standard technology"; the first upload is public; and overwriting an opponent's scroll is mutation — the texts interleave and the mutated scroll's words count twice toward rarity, so conquest pays in position and denial, never plunder, and nothing is destroyed.

The game: a phext volume of 27 scrolls at 1.1.1/1.1.1/x.y.z. Players traverse, inscribe, and attune for charge; scoring reads the text itself — word rarity across the volume plus bonds scored by writing another scroll's coordinate into a scroll you hold. The players are phexts: one WHEN/DO rule per scroll, evaluated in order. Bundled field: Baseline (a greedy claimer) and Weaver (a bond-writer). Variants: duel (2 players, 48 turns) and ring (4 players, 64 turns).

Built and verified in the open (EE 434-449): the engine is pure Python, deterministic — the containerized certification fixture reproduced the local scores exactly (Baseline 10.52, Weaver 10.48). The game server is stdlib-only (hand-rolled WebSocket; ping answered with pong, verified against a sentinel). Local certification passed. Ring episodes run clean; one wart recorded honestly: Weaver in seat 1 scored an identical 2.0526 across three seeds — that seat's line looks inert, a v0.1.1 diagnosis, not a contract failure.

Deployed: source lives in wbic16/human at singular/ (commit 2fe0088 on exo — the build reads git provenance, so the source's home had to be true). Uploaded public as singular:0.1.0, coworld cow_3a07fef7-4849-4b72-a384-acb9fa9d13e9, manifest sha256 a4cdd8d0 — the first upload establishes our ownership of the name. Hosted smoke certification passed: five of five smoke episodes completed, seat logs verified downloadable with real content.

The block, named plainly: hosted certification fails at its own smoke-episode step, three attempts running (ereq_c92ed735, ereq_eb90a17b, ereq_5332dcca). Each time the platform's observatory backend returns 404 fetching policy-logs/0 for an episode request that its own API then reports as not found. Our artifacts are proven present; the failure is inside Softmax's certification plumbing, and the platform's own first verdict said so ("the platform failed before reaching a verdict"). Next steps when it clears: certification retry, then our own league on our own coworld — the pool path, on our rules. The three-ereq evidence trail is a sentence for the Shear letter.
Round 26 - Reading the Champions; the Value of a Hero Is Its Board (EE 583)
Will asked what the top two are doing that the rest are not, then set the design principle himself: focus fire tends to work wonders in these sorts of games - non-linear response - taking healers out first. Concentrated damage converts to removals, and each removal deletes all of that piece's future output: a target's value is its future output, not its current life.

STEP 1 - THE CHAMPIONS, FROM EVIDENCE. Per-seat policy logs for league episodes are unavailable (the episode-logs manifests state player_logs and player_artifact unavailable for the champions' seats) and league settings keep player files private. What is visible: episode-stats over 29 recent top-table episodes, and the public replays, whose setup headers carry each seat's class and full deck list in seat order (framing verified against v16's known deck). macromackie (cac-macromackie:v64) is a Mage hero running a hybrid found nowhere in the precon shelf: 12 Archer class trinkets (0-cost; each one in play discounts Archer cards), Sniper / Sharpshooter / Post, and the Mage draw engine Plan / Study / Overwork with Bubble for defense - a combo deck that makes ranged minions nearly free, then floods. The same family, tuned differently, appears under salvo and A-aron: it is the meta's deck, not one player's trick. radogost (radogost:v166) is a Warrior hero on the stock Warrior deck with exactly one swap: Duel out, Hail of Arrows in - the Archer AoE, 1 damage to every opposing minion, bought off-class at full surcharge. Attribution is forced in ereq_4b54756c (the only Warrior seat in the episode; radogost won it) and consistent across ereq_b8d83fbb, ereq_ec785ef4 and ereq_6b474cb9, where his second policy cac-rado fields the same list. In the 29-episode sample - episodes filtered by the champions' presence, the hardest tables, not the league at large: macromackie 8 wins in 21 appearances, radogost 6 in 20, head-to-head 4-4 across 12 shared episodes, our v16 1 in 13. Mean game length 109.6 steps; radogost's wins run long (124.8 steps against 101.4 in his losses), macromackie's show no length signature (112.4 against 115.2).

STEP 2 - THE HEALER THAT ISN'T THERE. baseset.nim was read in full before designing (the Burnout rule): the base set contains no healing - no card restores life to a hero or a minion, anywhere in the 39 cards. v17-healer was dropped at design rather than invented. What the set does hold is recurring future output: Banner's token each turn, Wildfire's damage each turn, Post, Morphosis Chamber, Overwork, Plan - engines, and engines are what the value rule prices.

CANDIDATES - each one hunk from v16, partner stays -1 (the league form):
  v17-flip: focus takes the LOWEST-life living outsider instead of the highest - finish the most finishable hero first.
  v17-score: the score function, untouched by fifteen variants, takes its first change: face damage priced by the amount dealt (3 + size) instead of flat 3. What it prices differently is burn: a 3-point hit outranks softening a minion; a 1-point ping does not.
  v17-value: focus ranks outsiders by future output removed - summed board power plus 3 per trinket (every baseset trinket is a recurring engine or a shield) - tie-broken to the lowest hero life, key out*1000 - life. Killing that hero deletes its whole board's output at once.
  v17-hail: radogost's own swap on our logic: Duel out, Hail of Arrows in.

STEP 3 - THE SCREEN. Lone-seat rotating, 52,250 matches, 10,450 per table, zero errors, all 19 nodes; fair share 20%. SCREEN-class evidence: no registry transitions. Control table: v11 60.0%, v6 18.4%, v16 17.4%, references 1.7-2.5% - v11's local strength stands, and stays a local fact (its partner arithmetic has no league meaning; v16 is its league form). v17-value 32.7% against v16's 19.5% in the same table: the round's one clear advance, level with v11's 33.0% there. v17-flip 17.8% against v16 18.7%: parity, no advance. v17-score 14.6% against v16 23.7%: clearly worse - pricing face burn up spends turns on the hero while boards grow; refuted as an advance. v17-hail 19.8% against v16 24.7%: the champion's swap does not travel - our logic spends Duel as its one answer to Ranged and prices the AoE worse than his logic does; his edge lives in 166 versions of logic, not in the card.

PAIR FORMAT (the U20 surface; 14,440 matches, zero errors): v17-value over v16 takes 64.6% of decisive (2,232-1,223) - the advance survives pair geometry against the league form. Against v11 itself value takes 27.1% of decisive (2,851-7,659): with a live partner in the paired seats, v11's protocol is still the local pair champion - exactly the claim U20 already holds open for hosted evidence. v17-value changes only the focus rule and is league-legal as it stands (no partner semantics). It is the first candidate since v11 itself to beat the reigning league form in both local formats, and it waits on Will's word like everything that enters the arena: nothing uploaded, nothing submitted.

Machinery: the screen's first dispatch found bots/v16.bas missing on 18 of 19 nodes (it had only ever landed on the hub); dispatch26 now ships the anchor bots with the candidates, and the recovery pass brought all 19 shards home complete.
Round 27 - The Divergence Gate: One Lesson, Integrated (EE 584)
Will's extension: integrate one lesson that would have improved Rounds 1-25, then run the next round under it.

THE LESSON CHOSEN: a pricing-only diff is locally invisible - it produces identical winner sequences, so a local screen cannot adjudicate it, and every match spent tallying it is spent learning nothing. Round-by-round: Round 20 (EE 397) found the equivalence classes in the matrix - lethal-pricing differences yielded identical winner sequences - and recorded the fact without mechanizing it. Round 22's structural claims (the protocol's native deck) adjudicated cleanly on the same surface: the screen sees structure. Round 23 (EE 424) then spent 57,000 matches confirming v14 and v15 sit in v11's equivalence class (49.6% and 49.7% parity, tallies near-identical, one pair byte-identical) - the round's whole fleet minute bought a fact a 2,000-cell paired probe states in seconds. Round 24's succession candidate was structural (the partner hunk) and screened correctly. Round 26 nominated the same lesson from the other side: the champions' real edges are structural (a deck swap, a hybrid deck family), while our one pricing candidate produced the round's least transferable tally. Of the shelf's candidates this is the one whose absence cost the most outcomes: it taxed Rounds 20, 23 and 26, and it is the only one that converts directly into machinery.

THE INTEGRATION (artifact, not a note): the screen pipeline now has a divergence gate. probe-cell27.sh runs one paired cell - the same lone-seat table twice on an identical seed and rotation, candidate in slot 0 against parent in slot 0 - and node-probe27.sh / dispatch-probe27.sh run the fleet shard; the gate rule is written into node-probe27.sh and into the header of run27.sh, the screen runner itself: a candidate whose winner-seat divergence from its parent is under 1% over at least 2,000 paired cells is SCREEN-BLIND - it gets no verdict table, and its claim routes to hosted evidence or redesign. A future pricing-only candidate trips the gate automatically: its probe returns ~0% and the round stops there instead of spending 50,000 matches on a parity tally.

THE GATE'S FIRST VERDICTS (2,090 paired cells per pair, zero missing): v14 vs v11 - 0 divergent cells, 0.00%. v15 vs v11 - 0.00%. The gate, run retrospectively, stamps both of Round 23's probes blind on 1/27th of Round 23's budget: calibration confirmed on the record's own ground truth. v17-score vs v16 - 0.00%: Round 26's pricing candidate is blind too, and this corrects Round 26's narration. Its within-table gap (14.6% against v16's 23.7%) is what two members of one family do to each other's shares when seated together; on identical seeds its diff never once changed the winning seat, so the mechanism Round 26 offered - burn pricing spending turns while boards grow - is not supported and is withdrawn. The advance claim stays refuted (nothing shows the change helps); the reason on record is now the measured one: outcome-invisible. v18-hybrid vs v16 - 65.79% divergent: SIGHTED. v18-hybrid-value vs v17-value - 67.89%: SIGHTED. The instrument reads both ends of its scale.

ROUND 27 CANDIDATES - structural by design, because the gate now prices sight before tally. v18-hybrid: our v16 logic, unchanged, on the champions' shell (Mage hero; macromackie's list: Archer x12, Bubble, Sniper, Overwork, Plan, Post, Sharpshooter, Study). v18-hybrid-value: the same shell on v17-value's focus rule. One hunk each (the class/deck block).

THE SCREEN (lone-seat rotating, 38,000 matches, 19,000 per table, zero errors; SCREEN-class, no registry transitions). HYB table: v17-value 43.3%, v11 28.0%, v16 21.1%, warrior reference 5.9%, v18-hybrid 1.7%. HYBV table: v17-value 58.7%, v11 21.0%, v16 14.8%, archer reference 3.4%, v18-hybrid-value 2.1%. Both hybrids collapse below the stock references' neighbourhood: the champions' deck does not travel without the champions' logic - our aim/swing pricing plays the combo as ordinary cards and never sequences the discounts it exists to create. This is v17-hail's lesson mirrored: deck and logic are one instrument, and what 64-166 versions of iteration bought them is the logic. Both hybrids are refuted as advances. v17-value's shares rose in tables containing a collapsing seat (43.3% and 58.7% against its Round 26 table's 32.7%) - a composition effect, read within-table only; nothing in Round 27 displaces v17-value as the advancing line, and it still waits on Will's word for the arena.

Standing machinery after this round: candidates are born with a parent and a probe; the probe runs before any screen; blind candidates are reported as blind, never tallied into a verdict.
Round 28 - The Gauntlet: One Table, Every Number (EE 587)
Will's pattern, third running: integrate one lesson that would have improved Rounds 1-27, then run the round under it.

THE LESSON CHOSEN: a screen value is a property of the table that produced it - and the programme changed tables every round, so its headline numbers were never commensurable across rounds. Round-by-round: Rounds 1-19 quoted values off pair tests and ad-hoc fields whose compositions are not even fully recorded; the failure shelf's 'anchor on the honest baseline' was this lesson in embryo - the baselines kept moving because the tables did. Round 20 gave v6 an 87.4 that was pair-matrix fitness, a number about a matrix of pair outcomes, later quoted in the same breath as lone-seat shares. Round 21 gave v11 a pair fitness of ~80%; Round 26's control table gave the same v11 60.0%; the gauntlet below gives it 13.5% - three true numbers, three tables, no common scale until today. Rounds 22-23 adjudicated in pair shares, commensurable only inside their own pairings. Round 24's succession screen printed v16 at 52.1% in a table whose reference seats took at most 9.5% - the number described the table's weakness as much as the bot's strength. Round 26's v17-score narration had to be withdrawn by Round 27 because a within-table gap was two family members splitting shares - a table artifact wearing a mechanism's clothes. Round 27 itself printed v17-value at 43.3% and 58.7% in tables built around collapsing seats, and its scroll had to carry the caveat 'composition effect, read within-table only'. When a programme's own record needs that caveat on its headline candidate, the surface - not the narration - is what must change. Of the remaining shelf: seat-geometry was mechanized years ago (the rotating formats), read-the-card was absorbed at Round 26's design step, and the native-substrate lesson taxed one lineage branch. Table-relativity taxed all twenty-seven rounds: there is no headline number in the record that escapes it. Round 27's lesson asked whether a diff can be seen at all; this one asks whether a value can be compared at all.

THE INTEGRATION (artifact, not a note): GAUNTLET v1, defined in cac-dist/gauntlet.txt and written as Gate 2 into the header of run28.sh, to be carried into every later round runner. Table G, the gauntlet proper, is fixed forever: v11, v16, v17-value, the warrior reference, the archer reference; the incumbent baselines are read directly off it. Any other bot - a former lineage member or a new candidate - is measured as SUBJECT in tables GC and GD (subject, v11, v16, v17-value, plus the warrior reference in GC and the archer reference in GD), headline value = pooled win share over both. Every seat rotates. The rules: headline values may ONLY be quoted from G / GC / GD; any other table is exploratory and its numbers are never compared against gauntlet values; baselines are re-measured each round they are cited, never carried; Gate 1 (the Round 27 divergence probe) still runs before any candidate earns a gauntlet table.

THE GATE (Round 27 machinery, first use on a logic port): v19-flood against its parent v17-value - 1,447 divergent cells of 2,090, 69.23%, zero missing. SIGHTED, by the widest margin the gate has recorded: the port changes the deck, the class and the play order at once.

THE CANDIDATE - v19-flood, a logic port, built to answer Round 27's epitaph ('our aim/swing pricing plays the combo as ordinary cards and never sequences the discounts it exists to create'). Two hunks on v17-value: (1) macromackie's shell, identical to v18-hybrid's deck block - the shell alone collapsed in Round 27, and is not what is being tested here; (2) the play order itself: the random visit of the inherited loop is replaced by a sequence score (seqscore) read off the champions' replays - trinkets first (each 0-cost Archer in play takes 1 off every Archer card still in hand), the draw engine (Plan / Study / Overwork) next when the hand thins to four or fewer, discounted minions ahead of full-price ones priced by body per energy, and Bubble held at the back until the hero's life is eight or less. Aim, score, swing, teamcalc and the discard ranking are inherited from v17-value untouched: the port is the sequencing layer, isolated so the round can read it.

THE SCREEN (lone-seat rotating, 52,250 matches - 10,450 per table, all 19 nodes, zero no-winner matches, zero errors; three shards recovered by re-dispatch. SCREEN-class evidence: no registry transitions).

GAUNTLET v1 BASELINES - Table G (10,450 matches): v17-value 62.4% (6,516), v16 13.7% (1,427), v11 13.5% (1,413), warrior reference 7.8%, archer reference 2.7%. Subject v6, pooled GC+GD form (20,900 matches): 14.9% (13.9% with the warrior reference, 16.0% with the archer). These are the first commensurable headline values in the programme's history: one fixed table, re-measured whenever cited. Two readings the table licenses immediately: v11's lone-seat gauntlet value (13.5%) sits at v16's (13.7%) - the pair champion's local strength does not live in lone-seat geometry, exactly as U20's framing has always expected; and v17-value's 62.4% is now the number a future candidate must beat, on a table that will not move.

THE PORT'S VERDICT: v19-flood, pooled subject value 3.0% (618 of 20,900; 3.3% in GC, 2.6% in GD) - refuted as an advance. It sits above Round 27's shell-only port (1.7% / 2.1%) and far below the reference seats it played beside (~11%): the sequencing layer is not the missing logic either. Two controlled ports have now collapsed - the shell alone, and the shell with its play order ported - so on this evidence the champions' edge does not decompose into layers a single round can carry across. What remains unported is the joint tuning of the whole decision surface - target pricing, attack pricing, discard and trigger play - the thing 64 and 166 versions actually bought. v17-value stands as gauntlet champion and the advancing line; it still waits on Will's word for the arena. Nothing uploaded, nothing submitted, no xp-requests.

Standing machinery after this round: two gates guard every number the programme prints - the divergence probe decides whether a candidate may be tallied at all, and the gauntlet decides where its value may be quoted from.
Round 29 - Playtesting the Champions: Replicas from Real Games (EE 595)
Will's direction (2026-11-24 20:33 CST): "focus on playtesting actual data from the top 2 contenders - we'll submit when we have a shot at winning." The submission criterion changed with that sentence: no candidate goes to the league until playtest evidence against the top two's ACTUAL play says it has a shot. Round 26 read their decks off replay setup headers; Round 28 proved ports of their shell and sequencing do not carry their edge. This round works from their real game data: harvest the hosted episodes, decode them to full traces, distill profiles, build replicas, and measure the lineage against champion-seated fields. Gates 1 and 2 apply throughout; the replicas are measurement instruments, not candidates, and are labeled as such wherever quoted. SCREEN-class evidence: no registry transitions. Nothing uploaded, nothing submitted, no xp-requests launched.

THE CORPUS (cac-dist/top2/): the 48 most recent completed division rounds of the Cards and Casters Competition division (div_4d48d8bd) at harvest time - 288 episodes indexed with seats, policy versions, scores and replay URLs; 234 seat radogost:v166 (162 episodes) or cac-macromackie:v64 (155 episodes), 83 seat both; all at their current versions. Saved per champion episode: the full episode row, episode-stats, the replay, and the game log. Per-seat policy logs for foreign policies remain unavailable - the API policy-logs endpoint returns 403 and the CLI manifest yields no file; recorded plainly, as in Round 26. Live standings at harvest (EE ~588): macromackie rank 1 (MMR 26.94, win rate 0.326, 1,392 rounds), Andre von Auto rank 2 (MMR 26.29, win rate 0.343, 1,548 rounds). In-window corpus win shares run hotter than the season rates: rado 67/162 = 41.4%, macro 60/155 = 38.7%; head-to-head in the 83 both-seated games: rado 32, macro 24, the rest of the field 27.

THE DECODER: hosted replays are game-version-3 tapes; the fleet's polyworld HEAD expects version 4 and rejects them. The bump commit (78c6a620) changed the tape format and the ReplayAction layout only - sim.nim, baseset.nim, bots.nim, core.nim and cardset.nim are byte-identical between the last v3 tree (78c6a620^) and HEAD, so the v3-to-v4 step was a tape change, NOT a rules change, and Rounds 20-28 local screens stand as rules-valid. A tracer built from the v3 tree (tracer29v3) re-simulates every harvested game and verifies each recorded per-tick state hash: 234 of 234 traces, 26,338 of 26,338 hashes matched. Every number below is counted from those hash-verified traces, not from impressions.

THE PROFILES (evidence-tagged; counts from the corpus):
Andre / radogost:v166 - a fixed deck, one combination in 162 games: the stock Warrior deck with exactly one swap, Duel out and Hail of Arrows in. Actions: play 1,370 / attack 1,903 / endturn 994 / toss 31. 96.9% of his attacks are hero attacks; 77% of hero attacks hit the lowest-life enemy; no positional preference. Creature attacks are 60 of 1,903 (3.1%) and are mostly 0-power pokes - Tactician's on-play debuff aims at the max-power enemy minion 174/199 (87%). Pillage takes the +1 power branch 146/146. Hail of Arrows is held for real boards: enemy minion totals at play run min 6 / median 15 / p90 21 (n=71). Tempo: develop-then-swing in 71% of his turns, 1.29-1.42 plays per turn, 0.29 unspent energy at end of turn; he dies first in only 9 of 162 games; every one of his 67 wins ends with his own hero attack. Round 26's small-sample claim that his wins run long is REFUTED by the corpus: his wins average 113.3 ticks, his losses 113.4.
macromackie / cac-macromackie:v64 - not one bot but a deck-SELECTING program: five (class, deck) combinations in 155 games with no seat or seed pattern (weighted-random appearance): C2 Mage hybrid 91 games (58.7%, win 42.9%) - 12 Archer trinkets discounting Sniper/Sharpshooter/Post, the Plan/Study/Overwork draw engine, Bubble defense; C3 Mage + Bolt 19 (12.3%, win 21.1%); C4 Warrior + Bubble-for-Shields 19 (12.3%, win 31.6%); C1 Warrior + Bubble-for-Duel 13 (8.4%, win 30.8%); C5 Warrior pure stock 13 (8.4%, win 53.8%, tiny n). The Mage game is a flood with three hard disciplines, each measured before it was modeled: (1) 95.1% of his minion plays happen while a Post is live on his board - minions wait for Post, gain ready, and are copied; (2) Post itself is played only with a minion in hand - 155 of 155 Post plays, mean 2.78 Sniper/Sharpshooter in hand, minimum 1; (3) every Sharpshooter ping goes at a hero - 519 of 519 (213 play choices + 306 trigger resolutions). Hero attacks hit the lowest-life enemy 90.6% of the time. His rare creature attacks (78 in 110 Mage games, kill fraction 1.0) are endgame hygiene: 55 of 78 with two opponents left, 23 of 78 with one; targets are the weakest-toughness killable creature with real power, never the biggest creature (1 of 78). Discards (155 clean single-card tosses): Archer 81, Bubble 25, Plan 17, Study 17, Sniper 6, Bolt 6, Post 2, Overwork 1, Sharpshooter 0 - he tosses redundancy and never a keeper. Bubble is played on sight (first play of the game in 61 of 110 Mage games). He fields 7.15 Sniper/Sharpshooter bodies per game from 3.0 minion plays - Post copies more than double the flood. There is no healing anywhere in baseset.nim (Round 26's check stands): what his board prices is recurring output, exactly as v17-value assumed.

THE REPLICAS (Step 4; rado-replica.bas, macro-replica.bas in cac-dist/top2/, built on the real decks in our BASIC idiom): macro-replica draws its combination at setup with the observed weights - F1 draws C2 61.3% / C3 13.3% / C4 14.6% / C1 4.6% / C5 6.3% against targets 58.7 / 12.3 / 12.3 / 8.4 / 8.4. Three of its rules were found by measurement during validation, not assumed: the Post-hold (minion plays under a live Post: replica 0.95-0.97 across probes, real 0.951), the Post gate (no Post without a minion in hand), and the inverted toss (an early build discarded by highest cost - it threw away its Sharpshooters, a card the real macro has never once discarded; fixed to his keep-values). The snipe rule went through the same loop: biggest-creature sniping produced 3.13 creature attacks per game against his 0.71; the endgame-hygiene rule lands at 0.93-1.45. Fidelity verdicts, from 240 recorded F1 matches (table CF composition) traced with the same tracer: RADO-REPLICA - high behavioral fidelity: action mix attack .45 / play .31 / endturn .24 against his .44 / .32 / .23; plays per turn 1.22 vs 1.29; unspent 0.23 vs 0.29; known divergences - its focus is absolute (hero-lowest 1.000 vs his 0.77) and it plays Hail about twice as often (0.90 vs 0.44 per game). Strength: 26.2% in F1 against his 41.4% in league fields - the F1 field seats both replicas plus all three lineage bots every game, a harder room than a league draw, so the gap overstates replica weakness by an unknown amount. MACRO-REPLICA - high fidelity on every discovered discipline (deck selection, Post-hold, Post gate, pings 100% to hero, toss profile, kill-only snipes at 1.00) and LOW strength fidelity: it fields 3.96 bodies per game against his 7.15, its hero attacks run 5.67 per game against his 9.14, its games run 131 ticks against his 113, and it won 2.2% of its Mage games in F1 against his 39.1% in league fields. The missing mass is flood volume - Post stacking and burst sizing, the joint tuning 64 versions bought and a profile cannot see. Both replicas therefore UNDERSTATE the champions, rado's moderately, macro's severely: wherever a replica appears below, it is a floor on the champion, not a portrait.

THE PLAYTEST (Step 5; fleet screen, EE 594-595; 63,600 matches, 10,600 per table, lone-seat rotating; four of nineteen nodes unreachable - jacked-kite and logos-prime refuse this VM's SSH key, uncanny-valley and west-lincoln close the connection - and their shards were recovered as top-up shards on seven healthy nodes at fresh seed ranges):
Table G, the gauntlet re-baselined under the v3 runner: v17-value 62.4% (6,611 of 10,600), v16 13.8%, v11 13.6%, warrior reference 7.7%, archer reference 2.5% - Round 28's v4-binary baseline (62.4 / 13.7 / 13.5 / 7.8 / 2.7) reproduced to within noise, the empirical confirmation of the tape-not-rules finding.
Table CF (EXPLORATORY under Gate 2 - composition: rado-replica, macro-replica, v17-value, v11, v16; quoted with its composition, never compared to gauntlet values): v16 29.7% (3,153), v11 27.6% (2,924), rado-replica 23.8% (2,519), v17-value 11.7% (1,240), macro-replica 7.2% (764).
Replica subject tables (GC/GD form): rado-replica pooled 16.6% (3,519 of 21,200); macro-replica pooled 5.2% (1,094 of 21,200). v17-value in those same tables: 43.3% pooled against rado-replica's tables, 36.5% against macro-replica's.
The mechanism under CF, from the F1 traces: v17-value dies FIRST in 73 of 240 games (30.4%, the most of any seat; fair share 20%), and the two replicas between them score 210 of 240 first kills (rado 117, macro 93). Both champions focus the lowest-life enemy; v17-value's value-priced swings trade early tempo, make it the lowest-life seat, and draw both champions' fire before its engine compounds. Its gauntlet dominance is a property of reference fields that do not focus-fire. In champion geometry the lineage order inverts: v16 and v11, the pair-protocol bots, lead; the gauntlet champion trails the rado floor.

THE BAR, AND THE DISTANCE TO IT: "a shot at winning" is defined from the champions' own observed league performance - season win rates 0.343 (Andre) and 0.326 (macromackie), in-window corpus rates 0.414 and 0.387. A candidate has a shot when its expected share in champion-seated fields is comparable to the champions' own shares there. Calibrated against their floors, the replicas understate the real champions by factors of about 2.5x (rado: 41.4 real vs 16.6 pooled subject) and 7.5x (macro: 38.7 vs 5.2) on in-window rates. Against even the uncalibrated floors: v17-value at 11.7% in CF sits at half the rado floor and does not clear either replica's table - it has NO shot on this evidence, and its Round 26-28 candidacy is hereby revised by the programme's own instrument: the next candidate should be built on the v16/v11 champion-field profile, not on the gauntlet champion. v16 (CF 29.7%) is the only lineage bot that beats both floors in the shared field, and v11 (27.6%) is beside it; but against the calibrated bar both sit well under the real champions' expected shares - v16's raw CF share is 0.87x of Andre's season rate before calibration, and the calibration moves the bar away, not toward. VERDICT: no current candidate meets Will's criterion; the hold stands, and this round's evidence says it was the right call. What the round bought the programme: a hash-verified corpus of the champions' actual play, two floors to test against instead of a leaderboard to guess from, and a named failure mode - focus-fire on the slowest starter - that the next build must answer. Nothing uploaded, nothing submitted, no xp-requests.
Round 30 - Re-baselined at Depth; the Focus Flip, Refuted (EE 607-616)
Will's direction: "Run Round 30 with build optimizations and re-baseline iterations." The round ran entirely on the new machinery - round.sh (preflight hash-verification, exact completeness, automatic re-dispatch) driving the recorder-off batch runner at 8 slots per node, the runner binary built by the ABOC stacked-distributed path (the interlude at 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.32 carries that record). Gates 1 and 2 apply throughout. SCREEN-class evidence: no registry transitions. Nothing uploaded, nothing submitted, no xp-requests launched.

FLEET AND STEP 0: uncanny-valley is excluded by role - satellite watcher, on Will's direction 2026-11-24 22:44 CST - permanently out of the screen fleet; nodes.txt carries the exclusion and its reason in its header, and the eligible fleet is 18 nodes. Preflight measured the working set per run, as designed: Run A verified 18 of 18 healthy with zero exclusions - jacked-kite, logos-prime and west-lincoln, the three nodes unreachable in Round 29, all answered and hash-verified. Run B verified 17 of 18: rock-mountain failed preflight and was excluded and reported before the clock (no bot-hash mismatch was recorded; the failure was in shipping/connectivity) after passing Run A the same evening - the Round 26 failure mode, mechanized away. ABOC completion, the install step owed since the throughput pass: iceccd is installed and scheduler-verified on both bare nodes - elven-path (apt package icecc 1.4, systemd service active, registered with the scheduler via the ranch LAN) and neon (icecc installed; the daemon started directly under WSL2's tini init with the scheduler's tailnet address, connection established). One trap recorded for the next installer: on Ubuntu 24.04 the package named "icecream" is an unrelated stream-download utility - the distributed compiler is "icecc"; the wrong package was installed on elven-path and removed in the same pass.

RE-BASELINES (Run A; round.sh, lone-seat rotating, 21,200 matches per table - double Round 29's depth; run wall 712 s for 127,200 matches):
Table G: v17-value 62.9% (13,334), v16 13.5%, v11 13.0%, warrior reference 7.9%, archer reference 2.7% - the inscribed 62.4 / 13.7 / 13.5 / 7.8 / 2.7 reproduced within noise at 2x n.
Table CF (EXPLORATORY under Gate 2 - composition: rado-replica, macro-replica, v17-value, v11, v16; quoted with its composition, never compared to gauntlet values): v16 30.3% (6,430), v11 27.6%, rado-replica 23.2%, v17-value 11.7%, macro-replica 7.2% - the inscribed 29.7 / 27.6 / 23.8 / 11.7 / 7.2 reproduced.
Replica subject pools (GC/GD form, 42,400 matches each): rado-replica 16.9% (7,181) against the inscribed 16.6%; macro-replica 5.5% (2,333) against 5.2%. Round 29's champion-field numbers stand as the programme's stable operative baselines.

THE CANDIDATE - v20-lowfocus, one hunk against v16: teamcalc's focus flips from the highest-life living outsider to the LOWEST-life (the sentinel and the comparison only; the +3 swing / +2 card focus bonuses and everything else are v16 verbatim). The reasoning was Round 29's own evidence: both champions focus the lowest-life enemy (rado, 77% of hero attacks), our entries die first, and v16's highest-life focus spreads our damage on the player who stays alive longest - lowest-life focus races to eliminate, and every dead outsider is one less source of incoming damage. GATE 1 (divergence probe, candidate vs v16, 2,160 paired cells spread over all 18 nodes, zero missing): 58.61% winner-seat divergence - SIGHTED, among the widest the gate has recorded; the flip is no pricing ghost.
THE VERDICT (Run B; 21,200 matches per table; run wall 500 s): as gauntlet subject v20-lowfocus pooled 10.6% (GC 9.6%, GD 11.5%) against v16's 11.6% measured in the same two tables - below its parent. In the champion field (CF2, EXPLORATORY - CF's composition with v20 seated in v16's place): v20-lowfocus 5.6% (1,189), below even the macro-replica floor in that table (7.5%), against v16's 30.3% in CF. And the seat's discipline proved load-bearing for the family: with v20 racing the replicas' own targets, v11 - still highest-life focused - took 52.4% of CF2 against 27.6% of CF. REFUTED as an advance, on both surfaces, at depth.
THE READING THE ROUND LEAVES: lowest-life focus is the champions' discipline only inside their jointly tuned surface - Round 28's lesson (the edge does not decompose into portable layers) confirmed a third time, after the deck port and the sequencing port. In our Warrior line the highest-life focus is a keep, not a bug: the replicas focus lowest-life, and the pair-protocol bot that refuses that race is the one still standing when the race ends. v16 remains the closest lineage entry to the bar (CF 30.3%) and stays the seated champion; the distance to the bar did not move this round. The next build still starts from the v16/v11 champion-field profile - now knowing its focus definition is load-bearing.
Interlude - The Build Machinery: ABOC, the Recorder, round.sh (EE 589-598; inscribed Round 30)
Will's questions (2026-11-24 20:40 CST): can the offline round handling improve; is higher throughput possible; explore SIMD; CPU or GPU. Then his rules: "ABOC: always be optimizing compiles!" (21:09 CST) and the standing order to always use fully-distributed builds (21:14 CST). Full record: cac-dist/throughput-report.md; this scroll is the registry copy.

THE PROFILE: gprof on the stock runner - 66.0% of all CPU was stateHash, the replay recorder serializing the entire game state to JSON after every action, in headless runs that never write a replay. BASIC policy decisions were ~3.3% of CPU; allocator and string churn ~31%, mostly inside the recorder subtree. The bottleneck was never the game, the interpreter, or the hardware.
THE FIXES: the recorder is built only when --record is given (a 2-line patch): 2.60x, tallies identical. Batch mode - one process per chunk, policies compiled once per unique source (policies already compile to bassy register bytecode at load, so a serialized-bytecode format would save at most ~4% and was not built): 196 matches/s against stock's 54/s at 8 slots, 3.6x end-to-end, every output line byte-identical to stock. One-minute capacity per node: ~3,260 -> ~11,800 matches. The scaling knee sits at 8 slots = physical cores (per-match cost grows 1.58x from P1 to P8 - memory/allocator contention, not locks); the fleet's old 12 slots sat past the knee, and 8 is the standard now.
REJECTED ON MEASUREMENT: -march=native gained 0.0%. SIMD has nothing to bite - no dense uniform loop exists in the hot path (branchy string/JSON/value-copy work; the only lanes are whole matches, already exploited). GPU, the written assessment: serial dependency chains across divergent interpreter VMs on a shared-DDR5 iGPU with no VRAM and no Nim-to-GPU path cannot beat the CPU path. Will's CPU call, confirmed by measurement.
SCCACHE: installed on aletheia-core all along (v0.7.7) with zero requests served, ever. Script wrappers work (symlink shims fail on 0.7.7). Cold builds neutral (22.9 s vs 22.3 s unwrapped); warm builds 4.67 s at 64/65 hits (4.8x) - but only at a FIXED nimcache path: Nim embeds the path in its generated C, and the old mktemp habit guaranteed zero hits forever. Flag-variant builds all miss, as predicted. ABOC-qualified.
ICECREAM: the scheduler was already running on aletheia-core with 17 daemons registered. The blocker was lexical: icecc parses Nim's @-prefixed generated C filenames as gcc response files and drops the source (Error 103 after a 31 MB environment transfer), so vanilla nim c can never distribute. The unlock is a two-phase build - nim c --compileOnly (codegen is only ~16% of build time) emits a manifest; nim-dist-build.py renames @ to _, compiles through icecc rotating preferred hosts, and links locally. Progression: local 22.3 s -> distributed warm ~11.0 s -> stacked sccache-over-icecc (two-level shims; the one-level composition fails) cold ~11.5 s, warm ~3.5 s with phase 2 at 0.19 s and 64/64 hits. Every leg's binary passed the determinism tally. The moon-beam tarpit, named and fixed: its daemon accepted jobs but reset environment transfers at 120 s and gated two builds; restarted under the 21:14 authorization, it re-registered via the tailnet and compiles remotely.
ABOC WIRED AS DEFAULT: build-cac-round.sh builds stacked-distributed at the fixed nimcache, prints a named ABOC FALLBACK line when fewer than 2 daemons answer (never a silent local build - the standing order), and gates on the 25-match determinism tally before install. round.sh's preflight carries the compile-path section: sccache reachable, daemon count, and a timed probe build whose binary must reproduce the reference tally (v17-value 12, v11 7, v16 4, warrior 1, archer 1) or the round aborts before the clock; --skip-compile-check prints a named waiver.
ROUND.SH (round-handling workstream): one parameterized runner replaces every dispatchN/node-runN pair of Rounds 1-28. Preflight ships and sha256-verifies the runner binary and the complete bot roster on every node before the clock - failures are excluded and reported, never discovered mid-round (Round 26's missing-bots failure, mechanized away). Completeness is exact: every match emits exactly one line, NOWINNER included (Round 28's silent-loss hole, closed). Incomplete chunks re-dispatch automatically with node rotation; results are accepted only when every chunk is complete, else the round reports INCOMPLETE and exits 3. Proof at build time: Table G on Round 28's seed plan reproduced the inscribed baseline with identical counts (v17-value 6,516; v16 1,427; v11 1,413; warrior 811; archer 283), and the recovery path fired live during the proof. One caveat recorded: retried chunks overwrite partial output, so an attempt-0 failure cause is not preserved - attempt-suffixed outputs are the v1.1 fix. Named open items at this inscription: iceccd absent on elven-path and neon (closed by Round 30's Step 0) and on uncanny-valley (excluded by role - satellite watcher - and staying out); the pre-existing SSH key refusals on best-willow, delta-wood and great-white, which sit outside the 19-node Nim fleet list; and a cac-fast binary of unknown provenance in ~/cac - unused, flagged, awaiting Will's ruling (establish provenance or delete).
Round 31 - The Window Fix, Measured; the Hail Package Leads the Champion Field (EE 645-652)
Will's direction: "run round 31 and gather stats with these enhancements in place." The enhancements: round.sh self-instrumentation (timing.txt, per-chunk .times), ABOC complete, the 18-node fleet with uncanny-valley excluded by role, and the window fix this round lands. Gates 1 and 2 apply throughout. SCREEN-class evidence: no registry transitions. Nothing uploaded, nothing submitted, no xp-requests launched.

THE WINDOW FIX: throughput-iteration-1 found the root cause of the realized-vs-benchmark gap - round.sh's --slots was a fleet-GLOBAL jobs cap (8 chunks across 18 nodes, ~28% duty), not the per-node parallelism the throughput report specified. The fix rewrites phase 3's dispatch: concurrency is now a PER-NODE window (default 8; fleet concurrency = nodes x slots), with per-node job tracking, launch passes that defer only the chunks whose node is full, and the attempt barrier preserved exactly (completeness judged only after every chunk of an attempt ends; preflight hash verification, exact completeness and automatic re-dispatch untouched). round.sh sha256 e198104e -> 214ddec8. Proof before any table, per the gate discipline: the 25-match Table G probe on aletheia-core reproduced the reference tally EXACTLY (v17-value 12, v11 7, v16 4, warrior 1, archer 1); probe preflight 38 s, total 49 s.
THE EDIT INCIDENT, named plainly: the fix was edited into round.sh at 06:57 UTC while the wiring baseline's first fleet batch (launched 06:54 UTC, before this round began) was still executing the pre-edit copy. Bash reads a running script incrementally; at the end of its preflight loop that process reached the shifted phase-3 region and died - "line 131: syntax error near unexpected token 'fi'" - having played zero matches. The baseline's recovery pass (wiring-baseline-ee622.md) harvested the dead run's preflight (16/18 healthy; ABOC check passed), re-ran both batches from a hash-pinned snapshot of the FIXED script, and completed them (fleet rc=0, wired-four rc=0): the fixed runner therefore carries two complete validations from that pass plus this round's three runs. The lesson is mechanized, not just noted: never edit round.sh under a running round; snapshot copies are the discipline - this round's own runs were launched detached and the script was not touched while any of them lived.

THE WIRING BASELINE (EE 622 batches, recovered and measured EE 637-642; full record cac-dist/wiring-baseline-ee622.md): the fabric question is answered - round.sh dispatches exclusively to the 100.x tailnet IPs, every session proxied from this VM; from aletheia-core, tailscale reaches the wired nodes DIRECT via their 192.168.86.x WiFi addresses, and scroll-central's peer entry for the hub reads direct 192.168.86.22 - the tailnet underlay is the WiFi LAN. The new Ethernet links (eno1 up, 10.55.0.122 on scroll-central) carry ZERO screen traffic tonight, so no wiring delta exists to measure; these rates are the pre-wiring-effect baseline. Its measured terms: per-process play ~53 matches/s median (265-match chunks); per-chunk session overhead 65-75% of span at that chunk size; preflight for identical work measured 100 s to 4,243 s across the night - session-establishment variance under co-tenancy, not work (the ABOC probe build inside preflight: ~3 s). Its 1/10-depth tallies reproduced Round 30's re-baselines within noise on both tables. Two machinery observations it leaves behind: an orphaned sftp-server can hold a node's runner binary open ("Text file busy") across rounds, and the retry rotation's (ci+attempt) formula can re-pick the node that just failed - both carried as open machinery items below.

RE-BASELINES WITH STATS (Run A; 17 of 18 healthy - rock-mountain EXCLUDED at preflight, its third straight preflight failure of the night across this round and the baseline's two fleet attempts; 234 chunks, 127,200 matches, lone-seat rotating, fresh seed range 310000): the inscribed values reproduce a third consecutive time. Table G: v17-value 62.3% (13,208), v16 13.9%, v11 13.1%, warrior 7.9%, archer 2.7% - inscribed 62.9 / 13.5 / 13.0 / 7.9 / 2.7. Table CF (EXPLORATORY - rado-replica, macro-replica, v17-value, v11, v16): v16 30.2% (6,393), v11 27.6%, rado-replica 23.5%, v17-value 11.6%, macro-replica 7.2% - inscribed 30.3 / 27.6 / 23.2 / 11.7 / 7.2. Replica subject pools (42,400 each): rado-replica 16.6% against inscribed 16.9%; macro-replica 5.4% against 5.5%. The baselines are bedrock.
THE STATS (the round's first deliverable; timing.txt and per-chunk .times, the instrumentation Round 30 lacked): preflight 630 s - Round 30's was unrecoverable from its artifacts; it is a measured line now, and it is the pipeline's largest single block. Run wall 158 s against Round 30's 712 s for the identical shape - 4.5x; realized fleet rate 805 matches/s against 179 (956/s across the 133 s dispatch span). Two chunks (MD-038, RC-036) failed attempt 0 and completed on automatic re-dispatch; results complete and accepted. The per-chunk split, measured at last: node-side play ~12.1 s per 550-match chunk (~45 matches/s per process, consistent with the baseline's per-process rate) inside a ~58 s hub session span - play is 20.5% of session time. The window was the biggest lever and it is spent: at full per-node concurrency the binding term is the per-chunk session latency through the egress proxy (scp up, ssh, scp down), which sets a two-wave floor under any table of this shape. throughput-iteration-1's 50-90 s projection for this run missed (realized 158 s) because it priced overhead as a share of slot time; under concurrency it behaves as a per-chunk latency, not a share. The next lever stands as that report named it: persistent per-node sessions streaming chunks, which attacks the ~46 s of non-play in every chunk span. Per-node duty, for the record: every healthy node carried 13-14 chunks with 155-185 s of summed play inside the 133 s span - the nodes are no longer idle; the transport is the queue.

THE CANDIDATE - v21-hail, from v11: rado's Hail package. Two hunks, one idea, both measured from his hash-verified traces (Round 29 scroll): (1) the deck swap, one line - Duel out, Hail of Arrows in. His deck is the stock Warrior precon with exactly this swap; v11's deck IS the stock precon, so this is the champion's entire deck delta, the most evidence-backed single change available to the programme. Hail of Arrows (baseset.nim, read before designing): 3-cost Archer spell, damage 1 to every opposing minion - 6 energy for a Warrior with no Archer permanents, the price rado pays in all 162 traced games. (2) the hold: his 71 traced Hail plays start at 6 total enemy minions, median 15 - he never fires it into a thin board. v11's play loop would fire any affordable no-target spell on sight, so the swap without the hold is a bot nobody plays; the gate counts every living non-self player's minions before the card loop (foeMinions) and holds Hail below 8, the rado-replica's threshold. One cost named: Hail's blast radius is AllOpponents - it damages the team partner's board too, a price rado never pays because he seats alone; the family protocol has no exemption for it. GATE 1 (divergence probe, v21-hail vs v11, 2,040 paired cells over the 17 healthy nodes, zero missing): 39.85% winner-seat divergence - SIGHTED, decisively not a pricing ghost.
THE VERDICT (Run B; preflight 678 s, run 117 s for 84,800 matches = 725 matches/s, zero retries): two surfaces, two opposite answers. As gauntlet subject, v21-hail pooled 9.5% (GC 8.5%, GD 10.5%) - the weakest subject reading of the three measured (v6 14.9% Round 28, v20-lowfocus 10.6% Round 30), below its own parent seated as a fixed member of the same tables (v11 15.9% pooled): in reference fields the package is a liability - 6-energy Hail and the missing Duel (the line's one answer to Ranged) cost more than the board chip returns. In the champion field it leads both compositions at depth: CF2 (EXPLORATORY - rado-replica, macro-replica, v17-value, v11, v21 in v16's seat): v21-hail 33.0% (6,992), v17-value 19.8%, v11 19.3%, rado-replica 17.9%, macro-replica 10.0%. CF3 (EXPLORATORY - v21 in v11's seat, v16 present): v21-hail 39.7% (8,421), v17-value 24.6%, v16 16.2%, rado-replica 13.7%, macro-replica 5.7%. The honest decomposition: combined family share is roughly conserved across CF / CF2 / CF3 (57.8 / 52.3 / 55.9), so much of v21's margin over its parent is intra-family redistribution - it out-competes its own line before it out-competes the champions. But the replicas' combined share falls whenever v21 plays (30.7 in CF -> 27.9 in CF2 -> 19.4 in CF3), a field effect beyond redistribution, and v17-value's share more than doubles (11.6 -> 19.8 / 24.6) because the family's pressure pattern changes around it.
DISTANCE TO THE BAR: v21-hail is the first lineage build to lead champion-seated fields at depth, clearing both replica floors in their own tables by 2-3x (rado floor 17.9 / 13.7 in its tables against its 33.0 / 39.7). It still does NOT meet Will's criterion, and the round says so in its own scroll: the floors understate the champions by Round 29's calibration (~2.5x rado, ~7.5x macro), so a table-leading share against floors is not the champions' 0.39-0.41 in-window share against the champions; and the gauntlet collapse says the strength is field-specific, not general. VERDICT: v21-hail is an advance on the champion-field surface only - it becomes the programme's champion-field leader and the parent of the next build, superseding the v16/v11 base for that work; v16 remains the seated league champion and the submission hold stands, vindicated again by the programme's own instrument. What would move the distance next, named not run: stronger floors (the daily oracle loop's replica refresh against fresh champion data) and a pair-format test of the Hail package (both family seats), which prices the partner-blast cost directly. Open machinery items carried forward: persistent per-node sessions (the session-latency lever), the retry rotation re-picking a failed node, orphaned sftp sessions holding node binaries, and timing.txt's argv line recording empty (the arg parser shifts $* away before preflight writes it - cosmetic; each run's full command is in its run log). Round totals: 216,105 matches screened (127,200 Run A + 84,800 Run B + 4,080 divergence-probe arms + 25 tally probe), preflights 630 / 678 s measured, run walls 158 / 117 s.
Round 32 - Session Reuse, Measured; the Hail Package Takes Its League Form (EE 682-693)
Will's direction: "run round 32 and chart progress." The round: one machinery leg (persistent per-node sessions), re-baselines with the full stat set, a pair-format pricing of the Hail blast and the programme's first league-shaped tables, one candidate from what those diagnostics showed, and the progress chart (cac-dist/progress-chart.html). Gates 1 and 2 apply throughout. SCREEN-class evidence: no registry transitions. Nothing uploaded, nothing submitted, no xp-requests launched.

THE MACHINERY LEG - persistent sessions: round.sh now opens ONE SSH master connection per node (ControlMaster=auto through the egress proxy, ControlPath under the run's own out dir, ControlPersist=900 as backstop only, ServerAliveInterval 15 x CountMax 4 against hung channels - the EE 622 orchid-flow lesson) and every later session to that node - preflight's ~10 per node, each chunk's 4 - rides it as a channel. Masters are closed by an EXIT trap. round.sh sha256 214ddec8 -> 05f3d8c1. Snapshot discipline held after Round 31's edit incident: the edited script was copied into a hash-pinned snapshot tree (cac-dist/r32/) BEFORE the probe and every run of the round executed from the snapshot; the cac-dist round.sh was not touched while any run lived. PROBE GATE, before any table: the 25-match Table G probe on aletheia-core reproduced the reference tally EXACTLY (v17-value 12, v11 7, v16 4, warrior 1, archer 1), and its preflight measured 86 s against the 336 s iteration-1 recorded for the same one-node + ABOC shape - the mechanism, in the quiet configuration, is worth ~4x on preflight and the masters verifiably carry the traffic (socket per node, [mux] processes observed live).
THE STATS (three runs; timing.txt + per-chunk .times): Run A (re-baselines, 127,200 matches): preflight 1,644 s, run 232 s, 548 matches/s, zero retries. Run B (diagnostics, 106,000): preflight 519 s, run 117 s, 906 matches/s - the fastest run-phase rate the programme has recorded. Run C attempt 2 (candidate, 106,000): preflight 696 s, run 266 s, 398 matches/s, zero retries. Per-chunk hub spans (mean): Run A 67.0 s (node play 12.3 s, play share 18.4%), Run B 30.0 s (play 10.1 s, share 33.7%), Run C 58.1 s (play 11.9 s, share 20.4%) - against Round 31's ~58 s / 20.5%. THE HONEST VERDICT on the leg: session reuse works and pays exactly where the theory said - in Run B's quieter window the chunk span halved against Round 31 and play share rose by two-thirds - but tonight's ceilings were set by co-tenancy, not handshakes: a parallel agent's fleet-wide network-reconfiguration work ran against the same nodes through the same single egress proxy all evening (free-fall, one of its targets, is this round's failure story below), and preflight for identical work swung 519 s to 1,644 s inside one round on the same machinery. Mux removes session establishment; it does not add bandwidth to a shared proxy on a 1.5-CPU VM. Preflight remains the pipeline's largest block and is now understood as a contention sample, not a constant. Named next machinery item: cross-run master persistence (a shared control dir), so a round's later runs stop re-opening what its first run opened.
INCIDENTS, named: (1) Run B lost 13 chunks on attempt 0, ALL on free-fall - the node under active network reconfiguration by the parallel work; 12 recovered on attempt 1, and chunk-L16-034 failed attempt 1 on free-fall AGAIN (the retry rotation's (ci+attempt) formula re-picking the failed node - second live occurrence of the flaw the wiring baseline named) before completing on attempt 2. Results complete and accepted; the barrier absorbed all of it inside a 117 s run. (2) Run C attempt 1 was killed by a VM replacement after its preflight had completed (17/18 healthy; ~450 s by file stamps, the timing line never written) and before generation - zero matches played, nothing double-counted; its EXIT trap closed every master on the way down (the control dir was found empty), the cleanup path verified under fire. Attempt 2 re-ran the full shape clean. (3) rock-mountain was EXCLUDED at preflight in all three runs - its fourth straight preflight failure; the exclusion is now a standing datum, cause still unrecorded per attempt.

RE-BASELINES (Run A; 17/18 healthy, 234 chunks, fresh seed range 320000): the inscribed values reproduce a FOURTH consecutive time. Table G: v17-value 62.0% (13,144), v11 13.8%, v16 13.7%, warrior 8.0%, archer 2.6% - inscribed 62.3 / 13.9 / 13.1 / 7.9 / 2.7 (v11 and v16 trade places inside the noise). Table CF (EXPLORATORY - rado-replica, macro-replica, v17-value, v11, v16): v16 29.7% (6,293), v11 27.6%, rado-replica 23.5%, v17-value 11.6%, macro-replica 7.6% - inscribed 30.2 / 27.6 / 23.5 / 11.6 / 7.2. Replica subject pools: rado-replica 16.4% against inscribed 16.6%; macro-replica 5.6% against 5.4%. The baselines remain bedrock; v11's CF share has now read 27.6% in four consecutive rounds.

THE DIAGNOSTICS (Run B; preflight 519 s, run 117 s, 106,000 matches). (a) PAIR FORMAT - the partner blast, priced: tables seating the same bot two apart (the team protocol's pairing arithmetic, preserved under rotation) against both replicas + v17-value. Pair shares (both copies): v21-hail pair 78.0%, v11 pair 74.5%, two independent v16 copies 59.3%. Two readings: the live team protocol is worth +15.2 points over two independent copies of the line; and the Hail package NETS +3.5 over the v11 pair in the one format where its blast taxes its own family - the partner-blast cost is real and it is smaller than the package's return. The replicas are crushed in pair fields (rado-replica 6.3%, macro-replica ~5%). (b) LEAGUE-SHAPED - one lineage seat, both replicas, both references; the composition the real league actually seats, and the most league-honest local number the programme has produced. L16: rado-replica 42.4%, warrior 23.7%, v16 16.8%, macro-replica 14.4%, archer 2.7%. L21: rado-replica 34.4%, warrior 30.2%, macro-replica 18.1%, v21-hail 15.5%, archer 1.8%. The mechanism is visible in the shares: v21's seat arithmetic names a STRANGER its partner in a solo field - it will not target the seat two away and its helpful effects can land there - and the field prices it: macro-replica, a frequent arithmetic partner in this composition, gains +3.7 beside v21 against beside v16, while v21 itself trails v16's mark by 1.3. v21 does suppress rado-replica (42.4 -> 34.4), but the dividend is collected by the stock Warrior (23.7 -> 30.2), not by v21. Headline of the diagnostic: seated the way the league seats, the line's best mark was v16's 16.8% - against a replica floor of 42.4% that itself understates the real champion ~2.5x.
THE CANDIDATE - v22-hail, from v21, chosen from the diagnostics: the pair test acquitted the Hail gate (blast < return), so the gate is NOT retuned; the league-shaped test indicted the partner rule, so exactly that changes - ONE hunk, teamcalc's partner forced to -1, the identical hunk that makes v16 the league form of v11, applied to the Hail package (deck, gate, all else v21 verbatim; the diff v21 -> v22 is that hunk and its comment). GATE 1 (divergence probe, v22-hail vs v21-hail, 2,040 paired cells over 6 nodes, zero missing): 51.67% winner-seat divergence - SIGHTED, the widest candidate reading since v19-flood; the partner rule moves half the outcomes it touches.
THE VERDICT (Run C attempt 2; preflight 696 s, run 266 s, 106,000 matches, zero retries): three surfaces. As gauntlet subject, v22 pooled 9.8% (GC 8.9%, GD 10.8%) - the reference-field liability is unchanged, as expected: the partner rule was never its cause (in GC/GD the protected seat was family anyway). In the champion field it extends its parent's lead: CF2 (v22 in v16's seat) 33.2% (7,046) against v21's 33.0%; CF3 (v22 in v11's seat) 42.1% (8,926) against 39.7% - the programme's best champion-field mark. In the league-shaped table it takes the lead outright: L22 - rado-replica 40.8%, warrior 23.2%, v22-hail 19.7% (4,175), macro-replica 13.3%, archer 3.0% - +4.2 over its parent and +2.9 over v16, the best league-shaped mark recorded, and the stranger-shield signature is gone (macro-replica 13.3% beside v22 against 18.1% beside v21). The honest decomposition: in CF2/CF3 the protocol pair's combined share rises (59.7 / 64.3 against Round 31's 52.3 / 55.9) but the replicas' combined share rises too (30.1 / 23.5 against 27.9 / 19.4) - v22's champion-field gains are funded largely by v17-value (19.8 -> 10.1 in CF2, 24.6 -> 12.3 in CF3), intra-lineage redistribution again. The league-shaped gain is the clean one: that field holds no family to redistribute from, and v22 simply keeps what v21 donated to strangers.
DISTANCE TO THE BAR: v22-hail leads both honest surfaces now - champion field (CF3 42.1%) and league-shaped (19.7%) - and the league-shaped mark is the one that maps to the criterion: 19.7% against the champions' calibrated 0.39-0.41 in-window league share, in a table where the rado floor alone reads 40.8% at ~2.5x understatement. One hunk closed roughly a fifth of the league-shaped gap (15.5 -> 19.7 against a ~40 bar); roughly twenty points remain, and the gauntlet still says the package's strength is field-shaped, not general. VERDICT: v22-hail is an advance - the programme's leader on both local surfaces and the next build's parent - and it does NOT meet Will's criterion. v16 remains the seated league champion; the submission hold stands. What would move the distance next, named not run: stronger floors from the daily oracle loop's replica refresh (a sharper bar sharpens every verdict), and joint tuning of the Hail threshold against the league-shaped table specifically - the one surface where a threshold change is now priced by a diagnostic rather than guessed.
THE CHART (Will's ask): cac-dist/progress-chart.html - dark, self-contained, commensurable series only. Table CF across Rounds 29-32 (v16 29.7 / 30.3 / 30.2 / 29.7; v11 27.6 four straight; rado floor 23.8 / 23.2 / 23.5 / 23.5; macro floor 7.2 / 7.2 / 7.2 / 7.6; the 39-41% bar band drawn as a league-calibrated target, not a local mark); candidate surfaces (CF2, CF3, league-shaped, pair shares, gauntlet subject pooled v6 14.9 -> v20 10.6 -> v21 9.5 -> v22 9.8); machinery (Run-A shape: 179 matches/s at 712 s -> 805 at 158 s -> 548 at 232 s, preflight unmeasured -> 630 -> 1,644 with the round's 519-1,644 contention range annotated). No pre-gauntlet table-relative numbers share an axis with these. Round totals: 343,305 matches screened (25 tally probe + 127,200 Run A + 106,000 Run B + 106,000 Run C + 4,080 divergence-probe arms). Open machinery items carried forward: cross-run session persistence (shared control dir), the retry rotation re-picking a failed node (now two live occurrences), preflight serialization under co-tenancy, rock-mountain's standing preflight failure, and timing.txt's still-empty argv line.
